# Phase 3 results: MPSoC composition benchmark and interpreter v3

*Generated by `phase3/runner/analyze_phase3.py` from the <<prov.runs>> `metrics.json` files in `phase3/results/phase3_run/` (source of truth) and `compose_results.jsonl`. Do not hand-edit: change the template `phase3/runner/templates/PHASE3_RESULTS.md.tmpl` or the script and rerun. Every number below, including the numbers in the prose, is filled in by the script.*

## Design in one paragraph

6 models (llama3.2:3b, gemma3:4b, deepseek-coder:6.7b, qwen2.5-coder:7b, llama3.1:latest, qwen2.5-coder:14b) x 11 repair tasks (8 real MPSoC leaf modules + 3 seeded RISC-V/NoC modules) x 5 conditions x N=3 replicates = <<prov.runs>> runs, temperature <<prov.temperature>>, one shared budget of <<prov.max_iters>> LLM calls per run for every condition. Protocol `<<prov.protocol>>`: the defective input is evaluated first and the condition's feedback on it goes into the first LLM call. Every candidate is always scored with simulation + lint + synthesis; conditions differ only in what is fed back.

| condition | what the model sees |
|---|---|
| baseline | simulation feedback only; stops at the first simulation pass, **including on the unmodified input (0 LLM calls)** |
| raw_adv | simulation + filtered raw Verilator/Yosys warning and error lines; stops at clean_pass |
| sem_adv_v2 | simulation + production interpreter directives + the same raw lines |
| sem_adv_v3 | simulation + v3 interpreter directives + the same raw lines |
| sem_esc_restart_v3 | starts like baseline but behind a clean-pass gate (a generic "fails synthesis sign-off" message, no tool detail); escalates to v3 after 2 identical failure signatures; one blank-slate restart |

Metrics: `func_pass` (Icarus compiles, testbench prints ALL TESTS PASSED); `clean_pass` = func_pass AND structurally clean (Yosys succeeds, 0 latches, 0 logic loops, 0 Verilator MULTIDRIVEN/UNOPTFLAT/LATCH/WIDTHTRUNC); `silent structural pass` = func_pass but not clean. Sim-silent tasks (6): the defective input already passes simulation (MuxPC, execute_mux, mem_wb_mux, mux3, register_1bit, riscv_alu_flags). Sim-visible tasks (5): the input fails simulation (Address_select, ni_flit_pack, noc_xbar4, register_32bit, register_4bit).

**How to read the statistics.** Every rate is k/N with a Wilson 95% interval. Paired contrasts match runs on (model, task, rep), but the runs are independent draws (no shared seed), so the matching is a stratification, not a replay. Two views are given: an exact McNemar p on the discordant pairs (run level; valid for these specific (model, task) cells), and a bootstrap CI plus sign-flip p over the 66 (model, task) cells (cell level; respects that the 3 replicates of a cell are correlated). Table T12 shows the correlation is real: the cell-level CI is about <<dep.sem_adv_v3.ratio>> times wider than the Wilson CI, and most cells are unanimous. **Where the two views disagree, the cell-level view is the one to quote for a general claim.** An interpreter win counts only if clean_pass AND semantic_fired hold in the same run. Under the feedback-first protocol the interpreter fires on the defective input, so sf is <<pool.sem_adv_v3.sf>>/<<pool.sem_adv_v3.n>> for v3 and does not discriminate; the discriminating comparison is v3 against raw_adv, which shows the same raw text and has the same budget.

## Key findings

1. **A simulation-only loop signs off structurally defective RTL; running the structural tools removes most of that (T1, T2, T2b, T11).** Baseline makes 0 LLM calls in <<strat.silent.baseline.zero_call>> of its <<strat.silent.baseline.n>> sim-silent runs (it stops at the input) and ends with a silent structural pass in <<strat.silent.baseline.silent.fmt>>; over all tasks <<pool.baseline.silent.fmt>> of baseline runs pass simulation but are structurally dirty. With tools that falls to <<pool.raw_adv.silent.k>> (raw_adv), <<pool.sem_adv_v2.silent.k>> (v2) and <<pool.sem_adv_v3.silent.k>> (v3) of <<pool.sem_adv_v3.n>>. Sim-silent clean_pass: raw <<strat.silent.raw_adv.clean.fmt>>, v2 <<strat.silent.sem_adv_v2.clean.fmt>>, v3 <<strat.silent.sem_adv_v3.clean.fmt>>, escalation <<strat.silent.sem_esc_restart_v3.clean.fmt>>. On latch-bearing tasks, runs ending latch-free rise from <<latch.baseline.free_rate.fmt>> (baseline) to <<latch.sem_adv_v3.free_rate.fmt>> (v3); latch events on the final RTL fall from <<latch.baseline.before>> on the inputs to <<latch.baseline.after>> (baseline) and <<latch.sem_adv_v3.after>> (v3). *Scope:* baseline's 0 on sim-silent tasks is fixed by the protocol (an AutoChip loop stops at the first simulation pass), so this measures the value of running the structural checks at all, not a model-quality gap.

2. **Interpreting the tool output beats showing it raw, at lower cost (T3, T3b, T3c, T5, T10).** sem_adv_v3 <<con.v3_raw.all.rA.fmt>> vs raw_adv <<con.v3_raw.all.rB.fmt>>: <<con.v3_raw.all.delta_fmt>> points, cell-level CI <<con.v3_raw.all.cl_fmt>>, sign-flip p <<con.v3_raw.all.cl_p_fmt>>, v3-only/raw-only pairs <<con.v3_raw.all.disc>>. With the same raw text and the same budget, v3 changes LLM calls per run by <<cost.pct_calls_v3_raw>>% and tokens per run by <<cost.pct_tokens_v3_raw>>% (calls <<cost.v3_raw.calls.fmt>>, tokens <<cost.v3_raw.tokens.fmt>>, cell-level CIs exclude 0) and costs <<esc.sem_adv_v3.tok_per_clean>> tokens per clean pass against <<esc.raw_adv.tok_per_clean>> for raw. It also gets there faster: clean after the first LLM call in <<cum.sem_adv_v3.1.fmt>> of runs vs <<cum.raw_adv.1.fmt>> (raw) and <<cum.baseline.1.fmt>> (baseline). Production v2 also beats raw on sim-silent tasks (<<con.v2_raw.silent.delta_fmt>> points, cell-level CI <<con.v2_raw.silent.cl_fmt>>) and is neutral on sim-visible ones (<<con.v2_raw.visible.delta_fmt>>). *Not uniform:* after Holm correction only qwen2.5-coder:7b is individually significant (<<ladder.qwen2_5_coder_7b.v3_raw.delta_fmt>> points, Holm p <<ladder.qwen2_5_coder_7b.v3_raw.holm_fmt>>); llama3.1 is <<ladder.llama3_1_latest.v3_raw.delta_fmt>> and the 14B model is <<ladder.qwen2_5_coder_14b.v3_raw.delta_fmt>> (ceiling); per task v3 is below raw on mem_wb_mux (<<con.v3_raw_by_task.mem_wb_mux.delta_fmt>>) and ni_flit_pack (<<con.v3_raw_by_task.ni_flit_pack.delta_fmt>>) and level on noc_xbar4 (<<con.v3_raw_by_task.noc_xbar4.delta_fmt>>).

3. **The v3 upgrade fixes coverage; a gain in pass rate over v2 is not established at this N (T4, T4b).** v2 produced no directive on the defective input of register_1bit (<<up.register_1bit.sem_adv_v2_fired_input>>/18 runs) and ni_flit_pack (<<up.ni_flit_pack.sem_adv_v2_fired_input>>/18); v3 fires on both in <<up.register_1bit.sem_adv_v3_fired_input>>/18 and <<up.ni_flit_pack.sem_adv_v3_fired_input>>/18 with the intended class (<<up.register_1bit.sem_adv_v3_input_classes>>, <<up.ni_flit_pack.sem_adv_v3_input_classes>>). On register_4bit and register_32bit v2 directed <<up.register_4bit.sem_adv_v2_input_classes>> (the misleading class); v3 directs <<up.register_4bit.sem_adv_v3_input_classes>>. On the four focus tasks clean_pass is <<up_pool_focus.sem_adv_v2.fmt>> (v2) vs <<up_pool_focus.sem_adv_v3.fmt>> (v3): <<up_focus_contrast.delta_fmt>> points, cell-level CI <<up_focus_contrast.cl_fmt>>, p <<up_focus_contrast.cl_p_fmt>> (McNemar <<up_focus_contrast.p_fmt>>): favourable direction, interval includes 0. Pooled over all tasks v3 vs v2 is <<up_pooled_contrast.delta_fmt>> (<<up_pooled_contrast.cl_fmt>>). Per task: register_1bit <<up.register_1bit.sem_adv_v2_clean.k>> -> <<up.register_1bit.sem_adv_v3_clean.k>> of 18 (v2's clean passes there were <<up.register_1bit.sem_adv_v2_nosf_clean>> without any v2 directive; sf-gated wins v2 <<cls.ASYNC_RESET.sem_adv_v2.sfclean.fmt>> vs v3 <<cls.ASYNC_RESET.sem_adv_v3.sfclean.fmt>>); register_32bit <<up.register_32bit.sem_adv_v2_clean.k>> -> <<up.register_32bit.sem_adv_v3_clean.k>>, register_4bit <<up.register_4bit.sem_adv_v2_clean.k>> -> <<up.register_4bit.sem_adv_v3_clean.k>> (v3 returns to the baseline level of <<up.register_32bit.base_clean.k>> and <<up.register_4bit.base_clean.k>>, it does not exceed it); **ni_flit_pack: v3 fires correctly but clean_pass is <<up.ni_flit_pack.sem_adv_v2_clean.k>> -> <<up.ni_flit_pack.sem_adv_v3_clean.k>> of 18 (no gain).** *In-sample:* v3 was written from the real logs of these same tasks.

4. **Gains are largest for the 3B to 7B models; the 14B model is at ceiling with any tool feedback, llama3.1 (8B) gains little, and raw tool text can hurt a small model on defects that simulation already reveals (T6, T6b).** sem_adv_v3 minus raw_adv is <<ladder.llama3_2_3b.v3_raw.delta_fmt>> (3B), <<ladder.gemma3_4b.v3_raw.delta_fmt>> (4B), <<ladder.deepseek_coder_6_7b.v3_raw.delta_fmt>> (6.7B), <<ladder.qwen2_5_coder_7b.v3_raw.delta_fmt>> (7B), <<ladder.llama3_1_latest.v3_raw.delta_fmt>> (8B), <<ladder.qwen2_5_coder_14b.v3_raw.delta_fmt>> (14B) points, of which only the 7B model survives Holm correction. qwen2.5-coder:14b is <<ladder.qwen2_5_coder_14b.silent.raw_adv.fmt>> on sim-silent tasks under every tool condition. qwen2.5-coder:7b reaches <<ladder.qwen2_5_coder_7b.visible.sem_adv_v3.fmt>> (sim-visible) and <<ladder.qwen2_5_coder_7b.silent.sem_adv_v3.fmt>> (sim-silent) with v3. The smallest models remain lowest overall (v3: llama3.2:3b <<ladder.llama3_2_3b.silent.sem_adv_v3.fmt>> silent, <<ladder.llama3_2_3b.visible.sem_adv_v3.fmt>> visible). **Harm:** on sim-visible tasks llama3.2:3b scores <<ladder.llama3_2_3b.visible.baseline.fmt>> with simulation feedback alone but <<ladder.llama3_2_3b.visible.raw_adv.fmt>> with raw tool text (<<ladder.llama3_2_3b.raw_base.delta_fmt>> points, McNemar p <<ladder.llama3_2_3b.raw_base.p_fmt>>, uncorrected across 6 models); escalation restores it to <<ladder.llama3_2_3b.visible.sem_esc_restart_v3.fmt>>. The six models differ in family as well as size, so size alone cannot be credited.

5. **Escalation has the highest clean_pass and the fewest tool rounds of the tool conditions, but its gain over always-on v3 is not established, and how much of it comes from the generic gate message cannot be separated (T3, T5, T5b, T10).** sem_esc_restart_v3 <<pool.sem_esc_restart_v3.clean.fmt>> vs sem_adv_v3 <<pool.sem_adv_v3.clean.fmt>>: <<con.esc_v3.all.delta_fmt>> points (McNemar p <<con.esc_v3.all.p_fmt>>, cell-level p <<con.esc_v3.all.cl_p_fmt>>, cell-level CI <<con.esc_v3.all.cl_fmt>>). Tool-feedback rounds per run change by <<cost.pct_toolfb_esc_v3>>% (<<cost.esc_v3.toolfb.fmt>>, CI excludes 0), but LLM calls (<<cost.esc_v3.calls.fmt>>) and tokens (<<cost.esc_v3.tokens.fmt>>) per run are not lower (point estimates up, CIs include 0). Against raw_adv the gain is large and robust (<<con.esc_raw.all.delta_fmt>>, cell-level CI <<con.esc_raw.all.cl_fmt>>). <<escprof.noesc.n>> runs never escalated and <<escprof.noesc.clean.fmt>> were clean using only the generic sign-off message; the <<escprof.escalated.fmt>> runs that did escalate ended <<escprof.esc.clean.fmt>> clean at <<escprof.esc.calls>> calls per run. Of its <<escprof.clean_total>> clean passes only <<escprof.sf_clean>> are interpreter wins (sf and clean); <<escprof.clean_no_sf>> had no interpreter output. Escalation is strongest on sim-visible tasks (<<strat.visible.sem_esc_restart_v3.clean.fmt>> vs v3 <<strat.visible.sem_adv_v3.clean.fmt>>).

6. **Composition into the real designs: tools decide whether the designs synthesize at all, and leaf-level clean_pass was sufficient for composition in every case (T7, T7bounds, T7c).** The unrepaired references fail top-level synthesis (register_1bit's async-reset crash) with baseline at <<comp.mesh2x2.baseline.pass.fmt>> in mesh2x2, mesh3x3 and mpsoc, since baseline never touches that sim-silent leaf. Top-level synthesis pass (mesh2x2 = mesh3x3 counts, same three leaves): raw <<comp.mesh2x2.raw_adv.pass.fmt>>, v2 <<comp.mesh2x2.sem_adv_v2.pass.fmt>>, v3 <<comp.mesh2x2.sem_adv_v3.pass.fmt>>, escalation <<comp.mesh2x2.sem_esc_restart_v3.pass.fmt>>; mpsoc: raw <<comp.mpsoc.raw_adv.pass.fmt>>, v2 <<comp.mpsoc.sem_adv_v2.pass.fmt>>, v3 <<comp.mpsoc.sem_adv_v3.pass.fmt>>, escalation <<comp.mpsoc.sem_esc_restart_v3.pass.fmt>>. Reaching the golden bound (pass and no more latch cells than the all-golden repair): mesh raw <<comp.mesh2x2.raw_adv.at_bound.fmt>>, v3 <<comp.mesh2x2.sem_adv_v3.at_bound.fmt>>, escalation <<comp.mesh2x2.sem_esc_restart_v3.at_bound.fmt>>; mpsoc raw <<comp.mpsoc.raw_adv.at_bound.fmt>>, v3 <<comp.mpsoc.sem_adv_v3.at_bound.fmt>>, escalation <<comp.mpsoc.sem_esc_restart_v3.at_bound.fmt>>. Whenever every composed leaf was clean_pass, the composed design reached the bound: <<comp.leaf_predicts.mesh2x2.at_bound>>/<<comp.leaf_predicts.mesh2x2.n>> (mesh2x2), <<comp.leaf_predicts.mesh3x3.at_bound>>/<<comp.leaf_predicts.mesh3x3.n>> (mesh3x3), <<comp.leaf_predicts.mpsoc.at_bound>>/<<comp.leaf_predicts.mpsoc.n>> (mpsoc); no composition-only failure occurred. *Scope:* synthesis only, not simulation of the composed system; N=18 groups per cell, so the differences between v2, v3 and escalation are not statistically resolved (paired v3 vs raw at bound, mesh: <<comp.pairs.mesh2x2.v3_raw_at_bound.disc>>, McNemar p <<comp.pairs.mesh2x2.v3_raw_at_bound.p_fmt>>).

7. **What is left over is model capability and a functional-regression risk, not unfixed structure (T8, T8b, T9).** For sem_adv_v3, <<nonclean.sem_adv_v3.n>> runs did not end clean and <<nonclean.sem_adv_v3.structural>> of them still had a structural defect; the other <<nonclean.sem_adv_v3.functional>> failed to compile or to simulate (T9). Over all runs <<ce.total>> final candidates failed to compile, <<ce.by_model.gemma3_4b>> from gemma3:4b and <<ce.by_model.llama3_2_3b>> from llama3.2:3b, <<ce.by_model.qwen2_5_coder_14b>> from the 14B model; <<ce.fenced>> of them were well-formed fenced code blocks, so these are model syntax and declaration errors, not extraction failures. **Regressions:** no run was clean at some iteration and worse at the end (<<reg.clean_then_worse>>; runs stop at the first clean candidate). But <<reg.func_regress_total>> runs lost a simulation pass they had at some iteration, all with tool feedback: raw <<reg.by_cond.raw_adv.regress>>, v2 <<reg.by_cond.sem_adv_v2.regress>>, v3 <<reg.by_cond.sem_adv_v3.regress>>, escalation <<reg.by_cond.sem_esc_restart_v3.regress>>. Among runs whose input passed simulation, the final RTL fails simulation in <<reg.by_cond.raw_adv.broke_input.fmt>> (raw), <<reg.by_cond.sem_adv_v3.broke_input.fmt>> (v3) and <<reg.by_cond.sem_esc_restart_v3.broke_input.fmt>> (escalation). A keep-best-so-far rule would have restored func_pass (v3 <<reg.by_cond.sem_adv_v3.keepbest_func.fmt>>, escalation <<reg.by_cond.sem_esc_restart_v3.keepbest_func.fmt>>; a post hoc calculation on the logged trajectories, not a measured condition).

8. **Null and negative results (T2, T2c, T3, T3c).** On the sim-visible tasks, where baseline actually runs, raw tool text adds nothing over simulation feedback (raw <<con.raw_base.visible.rA.fmt>> vs baseline <<con.raw_base.visible.rB.fmt>>, <<con.raw_base.visible.delta_fmt>> points) and v3 is <<con.v3_base.visible.delta_fmt>> points (cell-level CI <<con.v3_base.visible.cl_fmt>>, includes 0). noc_xbar4 (MULTIDRIVEN) is at baseline level under every condition (baseline <<up.noc_xbar4.base_clean.k>>, raw <<up.noc_xbar4.raw_clean.k>>, v2 <<up.noc_xbar4.sem_adv_v2_clean.k>>, v3 <<up.noc_xbar4.sem_adv_v3_clean.k>> of 18 although the interpreter fires on all 18 inputs); riscv_alu_flags stays at <<up.riscv_alu_flags.sem_adv_v3_clean.k>>/18 under v3; mem_wb_mux and ni_flit_pack are below raw under v3 (T3c). The benefit is therefore concentrated in sim-silent defects and mid-size models; it is not universal.

